### When should you choose REST?

Choose REST when the caller needs the answer to proceed. A balance query, a funds check before a payment, or fetching data to render a screen all require the result immediately. REST is also the right choice at the public edge because browsers, mobile apps, and third parties speak HTTP and JSON.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.web.client.RestClient;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class BalanceQueryService {

    private final RestClient restClient;

    public BalanceQueryService(RestClient.Builder builder) {
        this.restClient = builder.baseUrl("http://account-service").build();
    }

    public BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId) {
        // REST is correct here: caller needs the balance NOW
        return restClient.get()
                .uri("/api/v1/accounts/{id}/balance", accountId)
                .retrieve()
                .body(BigDecimal.class);
    }
}
```

```text
// GET /api/v1/accounts/SAV2001/balance → 75000.00
// Caller blocks ~5ms, gets the answer, proceeds with the transfer
```

### When should you choose Kafka?

Choose Kafka when the producer should not wait and the interaction is an event, meaning a statement that something happened rather than a command to one service. Publishing to a topic returns immediately, and any number of consumer groups read independently.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.event.TransferCompletedEvent;
import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class TransferEventPublisher {

    private final KafkaTemplate<String, TransferCompletedEvent> kafkaTemplate;

    public TransferEventPublisher(
            KafkaTemplate<String, TransferCompletedEvent> kafkaTemplate) {
        this.kafkaTemplate = kafkaTemplate;
    }

    public void publishTransferCompleted(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        TransferCompletedEvent event = new TransferCompletedEvent(
                accountId, amount, "INR");
        // Kafka is correct here: fire and forget, many consumers react
        kafkaTemplate.send("transfer-events", accountId, event);
    }
}
```

```text
// Producer returns in ~2ms
// Consumer group 1 (Notification): sends SMS to Priya
// Consumer group 2 (Statement): appends to monthly statement
// Consumer group 3 (Fraud): scores the transaction
// Consumer group 4 (Analytics): updates the dashboard
```

### What are the costs of REST?

The cost of REST is temporal coupling. The caller blocks and depends on the callee being up and fast. A chain of REST calls couples the availability of every link, so you must add timeouts, retries, and circuit breakers. REST also does not naturally fan out: if five services care about a payment, the caller would have to call all five, coupling it to all of them.

```java
// WRONG: REST fan-out couples the transfer to five services
public void completeTransfer(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
    notificationClient.sendSms(accountId, amount);    // blocks
    statementClient.appendEntry(accountId, amount);    // blocks
    fraudClient.scoreTransaction(accountId, amount);   // blocks
    analyticsClient.recordTransfer(accountId, amount); // blocks
    auditClient.logTransfer(accountId, amount);        // blocks
    // If ANY of these is down, the transfer fails or hangs
}
```

```text
// Total time: sum of all five latencies (~50ms)
// If Fraud Service is down → entire transfer fails
// This is wrong because none of these need an immediate answer
```

### What are the costs of Kafka?

Kafka is eventually consistent and there is no immediate answer. Consumers must be idempotent because delivery is at-least-once, meaning a message may be delivered more than once on retries or rebalances. Tracing a flow across topics is also harder than following a single REST call.

```java
package com.bank.consumer;

import com.bank.event.TransferCompletedEvent;
import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class NotificationConsumer {

    private final NotificationRepository notificationRepo;

    public NotificationConsumer(NotificationRepository notificationRepo) {
        this.notificationRepo = notificationRepo;
    }

    @KafkaListener(topics = "transfer-events", groupId = "notification-svc")
    public void onTransferCompleted(TransferCompletedEvent event) {
        // Idempotent: check if we already sent this notification
        if (notificationRepo.existsByEventId(event.eventId())) {
            return; // Already processed, skip duplicate
        }
        sendSms(event.accountId(), event.amount());
        notificationRepo.markProcessed(event.eventId());
    }

    private void sendSms(String accountId, java.math.BigDecimal amount) {
        // Send SMS to account holder
    }
}
```

```text
// First delivery:  eventId=EVT-001 → SMS sent, marked processed
// Duplicate delivery: eventId=EVT-001 → already exists, skipped
// Idempotency prevents sending the same SMS twice
```

### How is REST different from Kafka? (comparison table)

The decision comes down to whether the interaction is a direct question or a broadcast announcement.

| Aspect | REST | Kafka |
|---|---|---|
| Interaction | Request-response to one service | Publish-subscribe to many consumers |
| Caller behavior | Blocks until response | Returns immediately |
| Coupling | Temporal: depends on callee uptime | Decoupled: producer does not know consumers |
| Consistency | Immediate | Eventually consistent |
| Failure handling | Caller fails if callee is down | Messages queue, consumed when ready |
| Fan-out | Caller must call each service | One publish, many consumer groups |
| Replay | No built-in replay | Durable log, consumers can replay |
| Throughput | Limited by service capacity | Buffered by partitions, scales horizontally |
| Best for | Queries, commands, external APIs | Events, notifications, analytics, sagas |

### What is the hybrid pattern for banking transfers?

The strongest pattern uses both REST and Kafka in a single flow. The critical path uses REST because correctness depends on it, and the side effects use Kafka because they are independent.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.client.AccountClient;
import com.bank.event.TransferCompletedEvent;
import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class TransferService {

    private final AccountClient accountClient;
    private final KafkaTemplate<String, TransferCompletedEvent> kafkaTemplate;

    public TransferService(AccountClient accountClient,
                            KafkaTemplate<String, TransferCompletedEvent> kafka) {
        this.accountClient = accountClient;
        this.kafkaTemplate = kafka;
    }

    public String transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        // REST: synchronous, caller needs the answer
        BigDecimal balance = accountClient.getBalance(from);
        if (balance.compareTo(amount) < 0) {
            throw new InsufficientFundsException("Balance: " + balance);
        }
        accountClient.debit(from, amount);
        accountClient.credit(to, amount);

        // Kafka: asynchronous, side effects happen independently
        String txnId = "TXN-" + System.currentTimeMillis();
        kafkaTemplate.send("transfer-events", from,
                new TransferCompletedEvent(txnId, from, to, amount));

        return txnId;
    }
}
```

```text
// Critical path (REST): check balance + debit + credit = ~20ms
// Side effects (Kafka): publish event = ~2ms
// Notification, audit, fraud react asynchronously
// If Fraud Service is down → transfer still succeeds, event waits
```

### What are common mistakes? (wrong vs correct)

Two common mistakes are forcing request-response onto Kafka and building long synchronous chains for fire-and-forget work.

**Wrong: using Kafka as a request-response channel.**

```java
// WRONG: publishing to Kafka and then polling for a reply topic
// This is forcing synchronous semantics onto an async system
kafkaTemplate.send("balance-request", accountId, request);
// Now poll "balance-response" topic waiting for the reply...
// This is slow, fragile, and misuses Kafka
```

**Correct: use REST when you need a synchronous answer.**

```java
// CORRECT: REST for immediate answers
BigDecimal balance = restClient.get()
        .uri("/api/v1/accounts/{id}/balance", accountId)
        .retrieve()
        .body(BigDecimal.class);
```

```text
// Rule: model commands and queries as REST, model facts as Kafka events
// "What is the balance?" → REST (query, needs answer)
// "Transfer completed" → Kafka (fact, many consumers)
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer models commands and queries as REST and facts as events. They resist two anti-patterns: forcing Kafka into request-response mode when a synchronous answer is needed, and building long synchronous REST chains for things that should be fire-and-forget events. They also know that Kafka provides ordering only within a partition, so they key messages by the aggregate ID (such as account ID) to ensure all events for one account are ordered correctly.

```java
// Key by accountId so all events for SAV2001 land on the same partition
kafkaTemplate.send("transfer-events", "SAV2001", event);
// This guarantees: debit event for SAV2001 is consumed before credit event
// Without a key, events could land on different partitions and arrive out of order
```
